22:53:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you’d think that—your experience is real: you see clouds of “steam” only when the pot is bubbling madly, never from a calm glass of water. That observation makes perfect sense, but it points to a subtle but crucial difference between **visible steam** and **invisible water vapour**. Here’s the key that clears up the confusion: ### What you’re seeing as “steam” is not actually water *vapour* – it’s tiny liquid droplets - True water vapour (the gas) is completely invisible. You can’t see it. - The white cloud you see above a boiling pot isn’t the gas itself; it’s millions of microscopic liquid water droplets that formed when the invisible hot vapour hit the cooler air and condensed. - So you don’t see evaporation happening. You see the *condensation* after rapid evaporation has saturated the air. ### Why a glass of water doesn’t make a cloud A glass of room‑temperature water *is* constantly evaporating – but very slowly. The invisible vapour drifts away and mixes with the surrounding air without ever becoming dense enough to condense into visible droplets. You can’t see it, but you can prove it: - Leave a glass of water out for a few days, and the water level drops. The water didn’t boil; it evaporated, invisibly. - Put a dry lid on that glass for a while, and you’ll find tiny droplets on the underside – water that evaporated from the cold liquid, then condensed on the lid. ### Back to the pot When you boil water, you’re generating so much invisible vapour so fast that the air directly above the pot becomes completely saturated. As that invisible gas rises and mixes with cooler air, it condenses into the visible mist you’re calling steam. That’s why the cloud appears only once the boiling is vigorous – before that, there’s still invisible evaporation, but it’s too slow to cause visible condensation. **So to your original question:** The sun evaporates water without boiling it because even lukewarm water sends off